#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "41b1a655-6092-57b6-8f95-396c566e7a83" :question/math-academy-id "e-21445" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=t^2+3t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =(t+2)^{2}+3(t+2)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t+4\\,+\\,3t+6\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+7t+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+2)} & =L{{t}^{2}+7t+12} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+7L{t}+12L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+12\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "88ee94b3-1f6e-587a-a63f-736f9df7186e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21582" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(9\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t+36\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡36\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡36\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221146 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1b27851d-3630-506e-972c-b6cef8dbeae3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21864" :question/problem "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-1)(t-1)^3 \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-1)(t-1)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a = 1.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-1) = (t-1)^3.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-1)$ with $t{:}$\n\n$$\nf(t) = t^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule $\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}{:}$\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^3\\} = \\dfrac{3!}{s^{3+1}} = \\dfrac{6}{s^4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor $e^{-s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-1)(t-1)^{3}} & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a82bf537-e6b4-513e-a4c8-69fc6a878c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{14}{s^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220170} {:answer/id #uuid "aba06933-95e7-5855-8767-609ef63cad94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} + \\frac{4}{s^{2}} + \\frac{1}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220171} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d8cad3-8704-5612-8e63-bc6c02fe05ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220172} {:answer/id #uuid "1cea79c8-83c1-59f7-987d-5cf603fb40ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220173} {:answer/id #uuid "68da82fc-f009-549f-81eb-94158587bd2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cea79c8-83c1-59f7-987d-5cf603fb40ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220173} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb63e8a-68c5-5d42-9a99-90049bceeae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "067ef9de-f25f-5573-a8ab-acd02fde3334" :question/math-academy-id "q-331016" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-5)({t}^{2}+4t+1)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-5)({t}^{2}+4t+1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-5)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-5s}\\,L{g(t+5)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+4t+1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+5) & =(t+5)^{2}+4(t+5)+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+10t+25\\,+\\,4t+20\\,+\\,1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+5)} & =L{{t}^{2}+14t+46} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+14L{t}+46L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+46\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-5)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-5s}\\,L{g(t+5)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5d5d6d6-f18d-56b4-a08d-5a93ec2af649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377035} {:answer/id #uuid "0e42a877-bc7a-541f-aca3-4ec658f3fb15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377036} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7f516a-0e0d-538c-b911-ac0b69a8f25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377037} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f4c672-5cb1-5208-b806-216782bb9953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377038} {:answer/id #uuid "e6734102-cdc1-5891-9391-309266f0b211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6734102-cdc1-5891-9391-309266f0b211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377039} :answer-field/id #uuid "33af0a24-78c8-52a4-867b-39ebd74ebc52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95b29307-c45f-5bdb-b3fe-c53fc83530de" :question/math-academy-id "q-331017" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-6)({t}^{2}-t+3)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-6)({t}^{2}-t+3)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-6)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-6s}\\,L{g(t+6)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-t+3.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =(t+6)^{2}-(t+6)+3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+12t+36\\,-\\,t-6\\,+\\,3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+11t+33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+6)} & =L{{t}^{2}+11t+33} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+11L{t}+33L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+11\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+33\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-6)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L{g(t+6)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45783918-e074-5533-a3dc-ea33abef94e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-8s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{-1}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377042} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0ba15b-670c-5df9-9bfa-c1b37461477f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377043} {:answer/id #uuid "91469f72-0093-5464-9ae8-6b654dd392f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377044} {:answer/id #uuid "8c980e2f-d04f-50d7-b03b-b9d4538ab213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377045} {:answer/id #uuid "b34e78b8-b5d5-50af-9443-45d1770fe403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca0ba15b-670c-5df9-9bfa-c1b37461477f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377043} :answer-field/id #uuid "e96a688b-bea6-59ba-80fd-1a2733953fca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d67ad842-c05f-51ce-8a9e-1454b4caf449" :question/math-academy-id "q-331019" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-8)({t}^{2}+5t-1)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-8)({t}^{2}+5t-1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=8,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-8)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-8s}\\,L{g(t+8)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+5t-1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+8) & =(t+8)^{2}+5(t+8)-1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+16t+64\\,+\\,5t+40\\,-\\,1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+21t+103.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+8)} & =L{{t}^{2}+21t+103} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+21L{t}+103L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+21\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+103\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-8)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-8s}\\,L{g(t+8)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{103}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9411e455-ab33-5f79-94d1-e7364e5c522d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377049} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4113db-3e81-5c2f-9df3-1f15e57ae188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377050} {:answer/id #uuid "a812825c-4b91-5372-9eca-b13df4980fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377051} {:answer/id #uuid "b05ca2a1-0de2-5f98-9699-154373df0b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377052} {:answer/id #uuid "bc35f188-d5c1-5f4c-b479-8af8312c8b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f4113db-3e81-5c2f-9df3-1f15e57ae188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377050} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2bf4089-2131-5330-943d-2eccfa4f85a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d50b6b30-ecc6-55ea-9bc0-857c1dec78ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-331025" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-11)({t}^{2}-2t+5)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-11)({t}^{2}-2t+5)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=11,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-11)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-11s}\\,L{g(t+11)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-2t+5.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+11) & =(t+11)^{2}-2(t+11)+5 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+22t+121\\,-\\,2t-22\\,+\\,5 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+20t+104.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+11)} & =L{{t}^{2}+20t+104} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+20L{t}+104L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+20\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+104\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-11)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-11s}\\,L{g(t+11)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed7df329-055e-58eb-867e-d0c24542222f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377056} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f5d077-34b8-5c17-bf9f-a9ed3b4fe878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377057} {:answer/id #uuid "92db6f5f-57a2-5438-9d0f-b00cdebc86fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377058} {:answer/id #uuid "78f9e325-e7a5-5ae0-9f56-efa11abeecac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377059} {:answer/id #uuid "39dc2c0d-1f92-5dfa-a0c1-25b17f08a645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3f5d077-34b8-5c17-bf9f-a9ed3b4fe878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377057} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cd77848-4a31-5628-bd38-832dc44172c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b7dd261-0c69-5613-9ae3-ff2b1fcb4deb" :question/math-academy-id "q-331026" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-14)({t}^{2}-5t+4)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-14)({t}^{2}-5t+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=14,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-14)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-14s}\\,L{g(t+14)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-5t+4.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+14) & =(t+14)^{2}-5(t+14)+4 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+28t+196\\,-\\,5t-70\\,+\\,4 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+23t+130.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+14)} & =L{{t}^{2}+23t+130} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+23L{t}+130L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+23\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+130\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-14)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-14s}\\,L{g(t+14)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ad8efce-d886-5225-ad71-5d3c0bf245bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{6\\pi }{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377063} {:answer/id #uuid "463e718e-3524-51e2-9319-d3f5b957295c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377064} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e6bbeb-f444-5967-862c-d6b32af19b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377065} {:answer/id #uuid "b0321413-db78-56f3-8cca-5f5b7f9e8e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{6\\pi }{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377066} {:answer/id #uuid "055a1aa0-13f4-561a-a527-d5faeb88587b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7e6bbeb-f444-5967-862c-d6b32af19b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377065} :answer-field/id #uuid "eec33360-e6da-51d1-b12e-b698cf07c21b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f80a38a-dd4d-5385-aec6-c2de845eb23e" :question/math-academy-id "q-331032" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+3) & =cos\\,(6\\pi (t+3)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t+18\\pi ) \\\\  & =cos\\,(6\\pi t+9\\cdot 2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+3)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+(6\\pi )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b165cc-79cb-56bd-9ce6-028f439f5a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377070} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe42840-be96-53a7-b91a-f72d9b222d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377071} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e66e90-a953-59cc-a9ba-d796578167a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377072} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e5d284-f41a-5cea-8761-170e9e603d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377073} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd8be68-0e59-58a7-b46b-7aa856286023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0e5d284-f41a-5cea-8761-170e9e603d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377073} :answer-field/id #uuid "e98b9e1c-0f10-5f0d-b9ba-9eff964a624c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00235b12-2ebc-5328-b8e1-a300837b18e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331039" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-1)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-1)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-1)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-s}\\,L{g(t+1)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+1) & =sin\\,(5\\pi (t+1)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t+5\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot (-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+1)} & =L{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-1)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-s}\\,L{g(t+1)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf8c9289-edd5-5405-8b75-0c79f50da573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377077} {:answer/id #uuid "b472ed5f-cc8d-5f71-bc56-c8a6f801da24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377078} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d6f365-5022-5fe7-b4d2-14b228d67f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377079} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba5c6b5-9879-5b79-85ae-cf8940aace39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377080} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9b2652-3281-5079-86a3-828e5a565d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c9b2652-3281-5079-86a3-828e5a565d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377081} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cee6fce-4d5f-5b6a-8fb9-ee2247f59094" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58e2b6d1-9c54-533b-96e8-140a01e86efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-331040" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-2)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =sin\\,(\\pi (t+2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+2)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1