$\n\nfind $L{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =sin\\,(5\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t+20\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡20\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡20\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82d1043c-f4e2-594b-a2d3-5a0e3bf57664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377091} {:answer/id #uuid "22b81cf8-5315-556c-b103-9a24b2d64080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377092} {:answer/id #uuid "63eec939-38e5-585c-a0cd-24757b942506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377093} {:answer/id #uuid "f58a3687-b9ad-51c6-9143-d0923df5c11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377094} {:answer/id #uuid "40a344e6-7011-551c-b5ac-6231b99e77df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82d1043c-f4e2-594b-a2d3-5a0e3bf57664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377091} :answer-field/id #uuid "14c203f8-0b78-55b5-a13a-96192bd89e0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5133eaa-2e71-5a14-8f05-9bddac5b59c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-331043" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+3) & =cos\\,(\\pi (t+3)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+3)} & =L{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4420a37-bef7-5a94-b616-62d8b9551ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-3s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220809} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d8c430-7310-5bdc-8a68-a73311588ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-3s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220810} {:answer/id #uuid "f002416c-3c62-555f-9c34-2e723ebfdb2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-2s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220811} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa4976c-774f-5e37-8c6a-599794ded913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-3s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220812} {:answer/id #uuid "36a3bcec-8570-5cae-955a-bc5fa95ff5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-2s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fa4976c-774f-5e37-8c6a-599794ded913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-3s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220812} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d39958c-d03a-5d44-9435-08be7071780b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d616ff98-be75-57e2-bce9-5627795650c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342318" :question/problem "Find $L{u(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)={e}^{-2(t-3)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{1}{s-(-2)}=\\frac{1}{s+2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2591dcd-93c5-5656-aa37-350a560ac32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377098} {:answer/id #uuid "4183603e-6b43-5fe5-a7ae-46bc68610604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377099} {:answer/id #uuid "f2282be5-5304-5abc-8345-7f906cd536cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377100} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6150df-446f-53bb-a790-8f035789b6b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377101} {:answer/id #uuid "a806298c-9601-5fb1-946b-0f664b15063f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2591dcd-93c5-5656-aa37-350a560ac32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0546e4f-2b43-55d5-8a96-4eed1f72fcfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e9f0adc-a25d-5538-9d91-d02b372d3966" :question/math-academy-id "q-342626" :question/problem "Find $L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=4.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-4)={e}^{-5(t-4)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-4)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{1}{s-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-4s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f46c5d7-828c-5100-bba6-c8f76601c8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377105} {:answer/id #uuid "729cc97a-76a6-5eeb-99bb-d57a89aaddc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377106} {:answer/id #uuid "4341dea8-4422-5a12-8fcb-7fd57b2c9e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377107} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2c6993-4eb3-5270-8757-155b1544aa47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377108} {:answer/id #uuid "cc09700e-29c9-557c-9733-4268dca07345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4341dea8-4422-5a12-8fcb-7fd57b2c9e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377107} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0f5766b-1400-59eb-adb7-46ef83d4e3f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident 